[SPEAKER_01]: I was always a starting pitcher.

998
00:59:12,860 --> 00:59:14,281
[SPEAKER_01]: George Kelly's your first baseman?

999
00:59:14,301 --> 00:59:15,362
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, George Kelly.

1000
00:59:15,562 --> 00:59:19,204
[SPEAKER_02]: He was there before Terry.

1001
00:59:19,724 --> 00:59:23,327
[SPEAKER_02]: Terry went to his, I mean, Kelly went to Cincinnati.

1002
00:59:25,144 --> 00:59:27,706
[SPEAKER_01]: He made the infielder's job a little easy with his height.

1003
00:59:28,146 --> 00:59:30,388
[SPEAKER_02]: Gosh, what an arm.

1004
00:59:30,548 --> 00:59:33,511
[SPEAKER_02]: He made all the relays in the outfield.

1005
00:59:34,412 --> 00:59:40,557
[SPEAKER_02]: Say an extra base hit was made, say right center, he went to right center.

1006
00:59:40,718 --> 00:59:43,600
[SPEAKER_02]: No use for him to be at first base because it was an extra base hit.

1007
00:59:43,960 --> 00:59:46,403
[SPEAKER_02]: So he went to right center and we cheated the ball.

1008
00:59:46,423 --> 00:59:49,886
[SPEAKER_02]: The guy who tried to score from first tried to score.

1009
00:59:50,666 --> 00:59:53,628
[SPEAKER_02]: He had such a good arm, an accurate arm, he could throw them out.

1010
00:59:53,888 --> 01:00:07,096
[SPEAKER_02]: And now if the ball was hitting left center, he would, when an extra base hit, he would go into back of second base out there and retrieve the ball and fire it up.

1011
01:00:07,116 --> 01:00:07,937
[SPEAKER_01]: So yeah.

1012
01:00:08,037 --> 01:00:10,939
[SPEAKER_01]: How many first basemen are used that way, are there?

1013
01:00:10,979 --> 01:00:13,180
[SPEAKER_02]: No, not the way, the arm he had.

1014
01:00:13,940 --> 01:00:16,462
[SPEAKER_02]: He had probably the best arm I ever saw.

1015
01:00:18,658 --> 01:00:19,499
[SPEAKER_02]: Pretty good hitter, too.

1016
01:00:19,679 --> 01:00:20,399
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, yeah.

1017
01:00:20,419 --> 01:00:22,281
[SPEAKER_02]: He's still in the latter part of his year.

1018
01:00:22,621 --> 01:00:25,203
[SPEAKER_01]: I think his eyes got a little hazy.

1019
01:00:25,263 --> 01:00:28,566
[SPEAKER_01]: I think he's still over in California, up to San Francisco.

1020
01:00:28,586 --> 01:00:30,087
[SPEAKER_01]: I hope to see him.

1021
01:00:30,107 --> 01:00:33,249
[SPEAKER_02]: He was working at the airport last I saw him.

1022
01:00:34,210 --> 01:00:35,271
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't know what he was doing.

1023
01:00:36,232 --> 01:00:37,192
[SPEAKER_01]: How about fielding?

1024
01:00:37,232 --> 01:00:39,354
[SPEAKER_01]: Did McGraw make you pitch his work on your fielding?

1025
01:00:39,414 --> 01:00:40,175
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah.

1026
01:00:41,356 --> 01:00:43,857
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, we had button games continuously.

1027
01:00:43,938 --> 01:00:47,020
[SPEAKER_02]: Have you been to any of these big, big games lately?

1028
01:00:47,532 --> 01:00:48,833
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, I watch them on TV.

1029
01:00:49,213 --> 01:00:51,435
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, you don't see the preliminary works.

1030
01:00:51,915 --> 01:00:55,698
[SPEAKER_02]: They get eight or 10 pitchers in a row.

1031
01:00:56,238 --> 01:00:58,720
[SPEAKER_02]: And they have a bunter that punts the ball.

1032
01:00:58,880 --> 01:00:59,521
[SPEAKER_01]: Pepper game.

1033
01:00:59,581 --> 01:01:00,682
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, the pepper game.

1034
01:01:01,542 --> 01:01:05,265
[SPEAKER_02]: So they catch it and throw it like this.

1035
01:01:06,686 --> 01:01:08,948
[SPEAKER_02]: And they throw it like a girl back to the hitter.

1036
01:01:09,488 --> 01:01:10,148
[SPEAKER_02]: And nothing.

1037
01:01:10,829 --> 01:01:13,251
[SPEAKER_02]: They might as well be standing up against the wall, you see.

1038
01:01:13,591 --> 01:01:15,292
[SPEAKER_02]: What we used to do is get three.

1039
01:01:17,180 --> 01:01:22,343
[SPEAKER_02]: button game, get the hitter far enough back so he could hit line drives at you, see.

1040
01:01:22,764 --> 01:01:31,089
[SPEAKER_02]: Now, wherever the ball was, we tried to field it and throw it back to him as a hitter like we'd throw it to a first baseman, you see.

1041
01:01:31,769 --> 01:01:33,290
[SPEAKER_02]: And we learned to field.

1042
01:01:33,551 --> 01:01:34,691
[SPEAKER_02]: She walked right by.

1043
01:01:35,472 --> 01:01:40,295
[SPEAKER_02]: Of course, I guess it was considered a good feeling for a pitcher.

1044
01:01:41,035 --> 01:01:42,036
[SPEAKER_02]: And I used to lead

1045
01:01:43,235 --> 01:01:48,378
[SPEAKER_02]: I could leave the league most every year and put outs in the sets.

1046
01:01:48,398 --> 01:01:49,699
[SPEAKER_02]: You had to be good.

1047
01:01:50,199 --> 01:01:52,440
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, I landed and I could go either way.

1048
01:01:53,641 --> 01:01:55,502
[SPEAKER_02]: And I never got off balance.

1049
01:01:55,782 --> 01:01:58,024
[SPEAKER_02]: When you finished your motion, you really sat down.

1050
01:01:58,444 --> 01:02:01,085
[SPEAKER_02]: I made a little hop and sat to see.

1051
01:02:01,246 --> 01:02:02,326
[SPEAKER_02]: My balls hit back.

1052
01:02:02,346 --> 01:02:09,110
[SPEAKER_02]: I used to kick them, stomp them, knock them down so they wouldn't go through the box if I couldn't get over.

1053
01:02:09,230 --> 01:02:11,772
[SPEAKER_02]: Did you ever get hit by a ball, batted ball very hard?

1054
01:02:11,872 --> 01:02:12,232
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah.

1055
01:02:13,323 --> 01:02:16,025
[SPEAKER_02]: I got knocked out twice in about 15 minutes.

1056
01:02:16,285 --> 01:02:17,046
[SPEAKER_02]: Is that right?

1057
01:02:17,066 --> 01:02:17,166
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

1058
01:02:17,326 --> 01:02:20,448
[SPEAKER_02]: I got hit in the groin and knocked out.

1059
01:02:21,729 --> 01:02:26,573
[SPEAKER_02]: And I picked the ball up and threw it through the first baseman.

1060
01:02:28,014 --> 01:02:29,495
[SPEAKER_02]: Got the man out with a third out.

1061
01:02:30,216 --> 01:02:33,258
[SPEAKER_02]: I went in and the umpire came over and said, you all right?

1062
01:02:33,358 --> 01:02:35,199
[SPEAKER_02]: And I said, yeah, I think I'm all right.

1063
01:02:35,480 --> 01:02:36,640
[SPEAKER_02]: He said, well, take your time.

1064
01:02:36,680 --> 01:02:37,281
[SPEAKER_02]: Take your time.

1065
01:02:37,801 --> 01:02:39,162
[SPEAKER_02]: So I did, and I'm the first hitter.

1066
01:02:39,943 --> 01:02:41,284
[SPEAKER_02]: We were playing against Brooklyn.

1067
01:02:43,334 --> 01:02:44,914
[SPEAKER_02]: First picture guy hit me right there.

1068
01:02:45,314 --> 01:02:45,995
[SPEAKER_02]: I didn't see it.

1069
01:02:46,035 --> 01:02:49,275
[SPEAKER_02]: He was right there in the jungle with a passport.

1070
01:02:50,096 --> 01:02:52,376
[SPEAKER_02]: Down I went again, and Lopez was a catcher.

1071
01:02:53,937 --> 01:02:57,517
[SPEAKER_02]: And it took him about two or three minutes, he said, to catch me.

1072
01:02:58,158 --> 01:03:02,079
[SPEAKER_02]: I'd get up and I'd start again, and I'd go down, and I'd get up and start again.

1073
01:03:03,159 --> 01:03:07,700
[SPEAKER_02]: So when I woke up, he said, I guess you've had enough.

1074
01:03:07,820 --> 01:03:10,361
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, tell you it doesn't matter.

1075
01:03:13,212 --> 01:03:15,232
[SPEAKER_01]: Van Lingo Mungo, pretty hard thrower, huh?

1076
01:03:15,492 --> 01:03:15,993
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah.

1077
01:03:16,313 --> 01:03:17,353
[SPEAKER_01]: He had a good fastball.

1078
01:03:17,373 --> 01:03:18,093
[SPEAKER_01]: He was wild.

1079
01:03:18,113 --> 01:03:23,114
[SPEAKER_02]: He was a little too wild.

1080
01:03:24,074 --> 01:03:28,755
[SPEAKER_02]: And he was wild off the field, too.

1081
01:03:28,775 --> 01:03:31,375
[SPEAKER_02]: That hurt him, yeah.

1082
01:03:31,716 --> 01:03:32,716
[SPEAKER_01]: How about Casey?

1083
01:03:32,756 --> 01:03:36,436
[SPEAKER_01]: Was Casey Stangle manager when you went to Brooklyn?

1084
01:03:36,696 --> 01:03:37,237
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no, no.

1085
01:03:39,916 --> 01:03:44,397
[SPEAKER_02]: No, he was, he went over to Boston, you know, and that was Boston.

1086
01:03:44,497 --> 01:03:46,678
[SPEAKER_02]: I never played around there much.

1087
01:03:47,598 --> 01:03:49,879
[SPEAKER_01]: How about your single biggest thrill in baseball?

1088
01:03:49,959 --> 01:03:51,679
[SPEAKER_02]: Anything stand out as a player?

1089
01:03:51,699 --> 01:03:53,580
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, going to the big leagues.

1090
01:03:53,960 --> 01:03:55,440
[SPEAKER_02]: Being sold to the Giants.

1091
01:03:56,200 --> 01:04:00,121
[SPEAKER_02]: Just the thought of being sold to a big league.

1092
01:04:01,061 --> 01:04:02,702
[SPEAKER_02]: And of course, that had to mean

1093
01:04:04,537 --> 01:04:05,718
[SPEAKER_02]: Making good, too.

1094
01:04:05,758 --> 01:04:09,120
[SPEAKER_02]: You're not just getting there, but you have to make good.

1095
01:04:09,720 --> 01:04:15,324
[SPEAKER_02]: So I made good, and I was a starting pitcher all the rest of it.

1096
01:04:16,004 --> 01:04:17,125
[SPEAKER_02]: So I was very fortunate.

1097
01:04:17,165 --> 01:04:23,729
[SPEAKER_02]: I was in six World Series, three as a pitcher, three as a coach, and two All-Star games as a coach.

1098
01:04:23,769 --> 01:04:26,330
[SPEAKER_02]: So I had a pretty full life.

1099
01:04:26,491 --> 01:04:27,911
[SPEAKER_02]: 36 years in the major leagues.

1100
01:04:28,112 --> 01:04:28,992
[SPEAKER_02]: Had a great career.

1101
01:04:29,252 --> 01:04:30,093
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes, I did.

1102
01:04:30,233 --> 01:04:33,635
[SPEAKER_02]: And I couldn't, you know, I can't take any of that back.

1103
01:04:34,915 --> 01:04:37,515
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I think that's a fitting note to finish up on.

1104
01:04:37,535 --> 01:04:39,756
[SPEAKER_01]: I want to thank you very much for a fine interview.

1105
01:04:39,916 --> 01:04:41,876
[SPEAKER_00]: It was wonderful.

1106
01:04:43,817 --> 01:04:47,237
[SPEAKER_00]: First, you should put on here about what happened.

1107
01:04:48,097 --> 01:05:02,220
[SPEAKER_01]: Here we are in our little motel room the same night, June 16, 1978, a night, a day we will not forget for a while.

1108
01:05:02,260 --> 01:05:03,440
[SPEAKER_01]: We were in Palm Springs.

1109
01:05:04,892 --> 01:05:06,993
[SPEAKER_01]: which is not very far from where we were last night.

1110
01:05:08,854 --> 01:05:11,015
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's quite a distance from where we thought we'd be tonight.

1111
01:05:20,720 --> 01:05:29,565
[SPEAKER_01]: About a quarter after 5 and arrived about a quarter after 11 in Beaumont and took a room in the Mountain View Motel.

1112
01:05:30,646 --> 01:05:32,927
[SPEAKER_01]: It was quite cool there because it was so much

1113
01:05:34,088 --> 01:05:35,969
[SPEAKER_01]: a couple of thousand feet above sea level.

1114
01:05:36,029 --> 01:05:39,991
[SPEAKER_01]: Compared to where we had been, it was a refreshing relief.

1115
01:05:40,011 --> 01:05:43,694
[SPEAKER_01]: But I went in and called up, first I called Leo de Rocher.

1116
01:05:44,494 --> 01:05:47,396
[SPEAKER_01]: We picked Beaumont because it was between three possible interviews.

1117
01:05:47,956 --> 01:05:55,240
[SPEAKER_01]: De Rocher in Palm Springs, Freddie Fitzsimmons in Yucca Valley, and Jack Rothrock in San Bernardino.

1118
01:05:56,701 --> 01:06:00,763
[SPEAKER_01]: As I prepared to make my call, I found out that Rothrock had moved to Visalia.

1119
01:06:01,632 --> 01:06:06,033
[SPEAKER_01]: which meant that we had located much farther west than we needed to have located.

1120
01:06:07,134 --> 01:06:10,675
[SPEAKER_01]: Then I tried to call DeRocher and found out he had an unlisted number.

1121
01:06:11,995 --> 01:06:14,216
[SPEAKER_01]: Then I called Fitzsimmons, and he wasn't in.

1122
01:06:15,136 --> 01:06:18,977
[SPEAKER_01]: He wasn't likely to be in, but his wife was in, and she didn't know much about anything.

1123
01:06:19,797 --> 01:06:25,539
[SPEAKER_01]: But she said maybe tomorrow morning, which would be today, after 10, they had something to do up till 10 o'clock.

1124
01:06:25,619 --> 01:06:29,620
[SPEAKER_01]: If we got there after 10, he might be able to see me for a while.

1125
01:06:31,710 --> 01:06:35,292
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, meanwhile, so we plan to do that.

1126
01:06:35,392 --> 01:06:43,716
[SPEAKER_01]: But in the afternoon, we drove over to Palm Springs here from Beaumont, which is about 25 to 30 miles in the heat of the day.

1127
01:06:44,256 --> 01:06:47,117
[SPEAKER_01]: And Palm Springs is hot because it's at a much lower elevation.

1128
01:06:47,137 --> 01:06:52,079
[SPEAKER_01]: It's probably, well, today here, it was well over 100 again.

1129
01:06:52,139 --> 01:06:57,222
[SPEAKER_01]: It's probably in the mid-90s now, I suppose, outside.

1130
01:06:57,722 --> 01:06:59,643
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's 820 in the evening.

1131
01:07:01,071 --> 01:07:17,099
[SPEAKER_01]: And so we drove over to Palm Springs to see DeRoche and finally found his house and his apartment in the Joshua Tree Apartments, 1400 East Palm Canyon Drive in Palm Springs, apartment 210.

1132
01:07:18,199 --> 01:07:21,421
[SPEAKER_01]: And I went up there, and there was a paper laying out in front of the apartment.

1133
01:07:21,461 --> 01:07:22,481
[SPEAKER_01]: Nobody answered the door.

1134
01:07:22,501 --> 01:07:29,785
[SPEAKER_01]: And I went down and spoke to an old gentleman lolling in the beautiful pool of this plush apartment complex

1135
01:07:30,665 --> 01:07:34,207
[SPEAKER_01]: He said Leo was out of town on a speaking engagement, wouldn't be back until Saturday.

1136
01:07:35,268 --> 01:07:41,752
[SPEAKER_01]: So I left my message with him, and we returned to Beaumont.

1137
01:07:42,852 --> 01:07:45,394
[SPEAKER_01]: Then this morning, we got up.

1138
01:07:45,554 --> 01:07:49,076
[SPEAKER_01]: We had to take the car in to get the oil change in Beaumont at the Ford dealer there.

1139
01:07:50,137 --> 01:07:58,002
[SPEAKER_01]: And we got it in there at 8 o'clock and asked him to check our transmission because it was giving us a little trouble yesterday.

1140
01:07:59,308 --> 01:08:01,970
[SPEAKER_01]: not shifting gears smoothly.

1141
01:08:02,010 --> 01:08:04,973
[SPEAKER_01]: We had to race sometimes to get up into the next gear range.

1142
01:08:06,474 --> 01:08:07,535
[SPEAKER_01]: So we had him look at that.

1143
01:08:07,595 --> 01:08:09,097
[SPEAKER_01]: And he said it'd take about an hour and a half.

1144
01:08:09,117 --> 01:08:12,700
[SPEAKER_01]: So we went and ate breakfast, and then came back about 9.30.

1145
01:08:13,321 --> 01:08:17,524
[SPEAKER_01]: We ate breakfast at a little restaurant, Carl's, not far from the Ford dealer.

1146
01:08:18,425 --> 01:08:19,846
[SPEAKER_01]: Then we got back about 9.30.

1147
01:08:19,866 --> 01:08:21,027
[SPEAKER_01]: The car was all ready to go.

1148
01:08:21,107 --> 01:08:24,891
[SPEAKER_01]: He'd changed the oil and lubricated it.

1149
01:08:26,176 --> 01:08:32,101
[SPEAKER_01]: We adjusted the linkage on the transmission so that was in good shape, drove it around the block so no problem there.

1150
01:08:32,601 --> 01:08:34,903
[SPEAKER_01]: Took 26 bucks from us.

1151
01:08:34,983 --> 01:08:47,674
[SPEAKER_01]: And we were on our way to Yucca Valley, which was probably about a half hour to 40 minute drive east of Beaumont, along Route 10, back in the direction of Palm Springs.

1152
01:08:47,694 --> 01:08:52,138
[SPEAKER_01]: Then we take 62 north, about 20 miles, up a big mountain.

1153
01:08:53,402 --> 01:08:56,824
[SPEAKER_01]: And the old car was puffing and puffing going up that mountain.

1154
01:08:57,585 --> 01:09:11,553
[SPEAKER_01]: We got there about 10 after 10 in the morning, or maybe around 10 in Yucca Valley, and stopped there at a gas station to try to find out where Tamarisk Place was, which is where Fitzsimmons lives.

1155
01:09:12,694 --> 01:09:16,836
[SPEAKER_01]: And he never heard of it, but he went in and had a map of Yucca Valley there.

1156
01:09:17,617 --> 01:09:19,318
[SPEAKER_01]: He and his assistant were poring over.

1157
01:09:19,338 --> 01:09:21,479
[SPEAKER_01]: They finally located Tamarisk Place.

1158
01:09:22,368 --> 01:09:36,552
[SPEAKER_01]: and explained to me I had to go down Elk to Onega, over to Amador, down to Golden Desert Drive, and over to Koala, then to Carlisle, and then down to Tamarisk.

1159
01:09:36,692 --> 01:09:46,635
[SPEAKER_01]: And we made it all right without too much trouble in the heat of the morning there, although the high elevation we had was still getting pretty warm.

1160
01:09:47,827 --> 01:09:52,850
[SPEAKER_01]: And we finally got to Fitzsimmons' house, and we were greeted by a couple of barking dogs.

1161
01:09:52,950 --> 01:09:58,273
[SPEAKER_01]: Practically every interview of this trip has been marked by barking dogs.

1162
01:10:00,254 --> 01:10:03,135
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, however, he came out into the garage.

1163
01:10:03,415 --> 01:10:05,496
[SPEAKER_01]: And this is my first garage interview in history.

1164
01:10:06,997 --> 01:10:11,660
[SPEAKER_01]: The garage was just even with the main part of the house, and the doors were open.

1165
01:10:11,680 --> 01:10:13,361
[SPEAKER_00]: There was a motorcycle in there.

1166
01:10:16,830 --> 01:10:31,280
[SPEAKER_01]: the usual garage arrangements, and all of his baseball pictures and mementos and things are in the garage, rather neatly mounted on walls and on tables around there, but it's not quite like having it all set up in an internal den.

1167
01:10:33,481 --> 01:10:35,483
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, so he was very nice.

1168
01:10:35,543 --> 01:10:43,588
[SPEAKER_01]: We set the recorder up on a little, on a bench there and began going, and at around, as I say, around 10.15 or 10.20 maybe,

1169
01:10:45,183 --> 01:11:00,410
[SPEAKER_01]: He said he had to go down to the doctor in Palm Springs sometime shortly after noon to get some shots, cortisone shots, I guess, or some kind of shots in his hand and shoulders.

1170
01:11:00,590 --> 01:11:03,131
[SPEAKER_01]: But he said, sure, he'd have an hour or so.

1171
01:11:03,231 --> 01:11:04,191
[SPEAKER_01]: And so we set it up.

1172
01:11:04,251 --> 01:11:06,512
[SPEAKER_01]: And it turned out to be a very good interview.

1173
01:11:06,612 --> 01:11:11,294
[SPEAKER_01]: Once he got warmed up, he just, as you can tell on the tape, he needed no real prompting.

1174
01:11:13,553 --> 01:11:20,914
[SPEAKER_01]: He had a little trouble picking the words he wanted to use at times to express himself, but he did a pretty good job and he told some good stories.

1175
01:11:21,214 --> 01:11:22,855
[SPEAKER_01]: He didn't have too much of a sense of humor.

1176
01:11:23,455 --> 01:11:24,235
[SPEAKER_01]: I thought he might.

1177
01:11:25,115 --> 01:11:32,237
[SPEAKER_01]: Most pictures of him have him smiling and quite jovial, but he did have some good jokes.

1178
01:11:33,537 --> 01:11:37,297
[SPEAKER_01]: He was very congenial during the interview.

1179
01:11:37,317 --> 01:11:39,998
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you want to describe the garage if you get through yawning?

1180
01:11:41,643 --> 01:11:45,685
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, well, it was an attached garage.

1181
01:11:45,805 --> 01:11:51,008
[SPEAKER_00]: And he had his drive-in in the top of the car, almost didn't clear the garage door.

1182
01:11:51,028 --> 01:11:53,249
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, the luggage rack covered our tent.

1183
01:11:53,269 --> 01:11:53,489
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

1184
01:11:53,529 --> 01:11:54,729
[SPEAKER_01]: It hasn't been used lately.

1185
01:11:55,190 --> 01:11:55,410
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

1186
01:11:56,390 --> 01:12:08,436
[SPEAKER_00]: And as you go, as you're standing at the door of the garage, looking inward, on the far right-hand corner was the door into the house.

1187
01:12:09,601 --> 01:12:12,862
[SPEAKER_00]: And then he had five baseball gloves up on a wall.

1188
01:12:14,423 --> 01:12:18,785
[SPEAKER_00]: And then there was an old-fashioned sideboard with a mirror on it.

1189
01:12:18,825 --> 01:12:23,587
[SPEAKER_00]: And he had balls and trophies and things like that on it.

1190
01:12:24,107 --> 01:12:31,891
[SPEAKER_00]: And surrounding that, on the wall, were four black bats with names on them.

1191
01:12:33,031 --> 01:12:35,432
[SPEAKER_00]: And then he had pictures all over the wall.

1192
01:12:38,715 --> 01:13:00,160
[SPEAKER_00]: Just as you entered the garage, on the left closest to the door was an old beat-up bookcase or shelf-like arrangement filled with envelopes that had all his baseball stuff on it and a bag that said Giants.

1193
01:13:00,240 --> 01:13:00,920
[SPEAKER_01]: New York Giants.

1194
01:13:01,100 --> 01:13:01,960
[SPEAKER_00]: New York Giants.

1195
01:13:02,040 --> 01:13:03,720
[SPEAKER_01]: Nice canvas bag.

1196
01:13:04,261 --> 01:13:06,141
[SPEAKER_00]: It was filled with autographed ball.

1197
01:13:07,442 --> 01:13:14,527
[SPEAKER_00]: And the balls he had out on the sideboard were his 200th game ball, an orange ball.

1198
01:13:14,547 --> 01:13:16,868
[SPEAKER_00]: The only time an orange ball was ever used.

1199
01:13:16,908 --> 01:13:18,610
[SPEAKER_01]: In 1941, they experimented with it once.

1200
01:13:18,910 --> 01:13:20,611
[SPEAKER_01]: It was not a very good experiment.

1201
01:13:20,691 --> 01:13:21,371
[SPEAKER_00]: It faded.

1202
01:13:21,652 --> 01:13:23,333
[SPEAKER_00]: And there was another ball there.

1203
01:13:23,373 --> 01:13:24,033
[SPEAKER_00]: What was that?

1204
01:13:25,454 --> 01:13:25,834
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

1205
01:13:26,135 --> 01:13:27,155
[SPEAKER_00]: So those were there.

1206
01:13:28,916 --> 01:13:30,297
[SPEAKER_00]: And then his dogs.

1207
01:13:31,898 --> 01:13:37,182
[SPEAKER_00]: Since he had this arrangement with two seats, I either had a choice of standing

1208
01:13:37,753 --> 01:13:38,834
[SPEAKER_00]: or sitting in the car.

1209
01:13:40,375 --> 01:13:42,857
[SPEAKER_00]: And he said I could have his chair.

1210
01:13:44,257 --> 01:13:48,520
[SPEAKER_00]: But since he just had a knee operation, I didn't think that was a very polite thing to do.

1211
01:13:49,981 --> 01:13:53,504
[SPEAKER_00]: So I thought, well, I will trot around and see what I can see.

1212
01:13:53,704 --> 01:14:01,970
[SPEAKER_00]: And on the porch, they had those white metal scrolly chairs, Mediterranean type patio things.

1213
01:14:03,010 --> 01:14:05,512
[SPEAKER_00]: And I thought I'd go up there with my book and read.

1214
01:14:06,690 --> 01:14:13,793
[SPEAKER_00]: And I went up there and sat down and nearly jumped out of my skin when I heard this ferocious bark right behind me.

1215
01:14:14,893 --> 01:14:19,195
[SPEAKER_00]: And I got up and I turned around and I was staring into the eyes of a great dame.

1216
01:14:19,896 --> 01:14:21,176
[SPEAKER_01]: Great dame.

1217
01:14:21,396 --> 01:14:23,337
[SPEAKER_00]: Who was inside the house, fortunately.

1218
01:14:24,277 --> 01:14:25,838
[SPEAKER_00]: And it seems he has four of them.

1219
01:14:27,599 --> 01:14:29,420
[SPEAKER_00]: Two big ones and two puppies.

1220
01:14:30,720 --> 01:14:35,342
[SPEAKER_00]: And even he said he was afraid to have us in the house, whether that was just an excuse or not.

1221
01:14:35,922 --> 01:14:38,484
[SPEAKER_00]: Because I thought, boy, she must be some kind of madam.

1222
01:14:39,044 --> 01:14:43,688
[SPEAKER_00]: Wasn't she the one that said they had just moved in there and she didn't know how to?

1223
01:14:43,708 --> 01:14:46,750
[SPEAKER_01]: All her stuff was in storage or something.

1224
01:14:46,870 --> 01:14:55,617
[SPEAKER_01]: When I mentioned yesterday on the phone about scrapbooks and pictures, she had said that they had just moved in and the stuff was not out or available.

1225
01:14:56,238 --> 01:15:00,021
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, if they had just moved in, they were pretty well.

1226
01:15:00,061 --> 01:15:04,384
[SPEAKER_00]: I figured she just wouldn't have half that stuff in her house, is what I figured.

1227
01:15:05,024 --> 01:15:06,504
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why it was all out there.

1228
01:15:06,804 --> 01:15:08,005
[SPEAKER_01]: We never met her, by the way.

1229
01:15:08,045 --> 01:15:10,505
[SPEAKER_01]: She never appeared at all.

1230
01:15:10,525 --> 01:15:12,006
[SPEAKER_01]: She was in with the Great Danes.

1231
01:15:12,306 --> 01:15:15,286
[SPEAKER_00]: And a lady friend came and called on her and went in and talked to her.

1232
01:15:15,646 --> 01:15:17,047
[SPEAKER_01]: What did the Danes do about that?

1233
01:15:17,247 --> 01:15:20,047
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know, but she was in the house most of the time you were there.

1234
01:15:21,147 --> 01:15:23,288
[SPEAKER_00]: And the motorcycle belonged to his grandson.

1235
01:15:23,688 --> 01:15:25,128
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, I didn't think he drove that.

1236
01:15:25,928 --> 01:15:27,949
[SPEAKER_00]: Unless his grandson lives with him.

1237
01:15:28,029 --> 01:15:28,509
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know.

1238
01:15:28,589 --> 01:15:31,050
[SPEAKER_00]: I couldn't quite figure that out either.

1239
01:15:31,090 --> 01:15:32,810
[SPEAKER_00]: But he said they had moved up there

1240
01:15:33,303 --> 01:15:35,184
[SPEAKER_00]: because they had lived in Arcadia.

1241
01:15:36,004 --> 01:15:39,346
[SPEAKER_00]: And if they had lived in Arcadia, he would have been dead.

1242
01:15:40,206 --> 01:15:41,346
[SPEAKER_00]: So they moved up there.

1243
01:15:41,366 --> 01:15:42,827
[SPEAKER_01]: Because of?

1244
01:15:42,867 --> 01:15:43,527
[SPEAKER_00]: The smog.

1245
01:15:44,408 --> 01:15:52,491
[SPEAKER_00]: And they moved up there where the air was pure, and the water was pure, and he felt like a new man.

1246
01:15:55,412 --> 01:15:57,133
[SPEAKER_00]: I think that's my comment on him.

1247
01:15:58,333 --> 01:15:59,614
[SPEAKER_01]: Very good comment.

1248
01:16:01,232 --> 01:16:02,613
[SPEAKER_00]: That was a big dog, huh?

1249
01:16:02,653 --> 01:16:03,634
[SPEAKER_00]: Describe the dog?

1250
01:16:04,355 --> 01:16:07,097
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, just a big Great Dane, but it was the size.

1251
01:16:07,157 --> 01:16:08,738
[SPEAKER_00]: He was a big Great Dane.

1252
01:16:09,038 --> 01:16:18,126
[SPEAKER_01]: We were speculating as to why they had so many dogs, and we thought probably security, but I would think that's a pretty secure place up there.

1253
01:16:18,766 --> 01:16:24,211
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know, but I think you'd only need one of those, because I'm sure that he stood a good four.

1254
01:16:24,751 --> 01:16:28,234
[SPEAKER_00]: The top of his head was a good four feet high, not higher.

1255
01:16:30,303 --> 01:16:32,944
[SPEAKER_00]: As I say, I felt like I was staring him in the face.

1256
01:16:34,584 --> 01:16:38,065
[SPEAKER_00]: And so I took a chair and moved it over into the garage.

1257
01:16:39,646 --> 01:16:40,326
[SPEAKER_00]: Very good.

1258
01:16:40,346 --> 01:16:41,606
[SPEAKER_00]: And then I moved it back.

1259
01:16:46,268 --> 01:16:48,789
[SPEAKER_00]: And the interview was over.

1260
01:16:50,629 --> 01:16:59,452
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, we chatted on about one side of the tape ran out, and we were still going strong.

1261
01:17:00,905 --> 01:17:09,909
[SPEAKER_01]: I asked him if he wouldn't mind doing a little bit more, so I flipped it over, and just at the time of the... What story was he telling about somebody?

1262
01:17:10,069 --> 01:17:15,071
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, Kyler, when he decked him.

1263
01:17:15,131 --> 01:17:16,912
[SPEAKER_01]: He told that, but the tape had already run out.

1264
01:17:17,352 --> 01:17:26,817
[SPEAKER_01]: It was one of those days the switch didn't snap off on the recorder, and I had to keep an eye and make sure I wasn't running over, and I did run over a little bit, so he repeated that story.

1265
01:17:26,837 --> 01:17:28,978
[SPEAKER_01]: And it ran on for about another 20 minutes or so,

1266
01:17:30,011 --> 01:17:32,091
[SPEAKER_01]: Those kids out in the street, what were they doing?

1267
01:17:32,111 --> 01:17:33,072
[SPEAKER_01]: Making that clang, clang?

1268
01:17:33,112 --> 01:17:34,492
[SPEAKER_00]: They lived next door to them.

1269
01:17:34,572 --> 01:17:35,672
[SPEAKER_00]: There were four of them.

1270
01:17:36,032 --> 01:17:38,653
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know if they were all in the one family.

1271
01:17:38,693 --> 01:17:41,493
[SPEAKER_00]: And they were playing, and they were climbing up the hills and that.

1272
01:17:41,513 --> 01:17:43,273
[SPEAKER_00]: And then their mother brought them a treat.

1273
01:17:44,254 --> 01:17:47,054
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever it was, misty or something, I thought she said.

1274
01:17:48,074 --> 01:17:52,395
[SPEAKER_00]: And then pretty soon, out came this band, these instruments.

1275
01:17:52,455 --> 01:17:53,535
[SPEAKER_00]: And that was a cymbal.

1276
01:17:53,595 --> 01:17:58,316
[SPEAKER_00]: This little boy had two cymbals that he was clanging together that you heard.

1277
01:17:59,073 --> 01:18:00,835
[SPEAKER_00]: And another one had drumsticks.

1278
01:18:01,655 --> 01:18:04,678
[SPEAKER_01]: That's short in the interview, that racket in the background.

1279
01:18:04,938 --> 01:18:06,459
[SPEAKER_01]: I know we couldn't go on much longer with that.

1280
01:18:06,479 --> 01:18:07,300
[SPEAKER_01]: But it was running out.

1281
01:18:07,380 --> 01:18:08,661
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm glad they didn't come any earlier.

1282
01:18:10,122 --> 01:18:11,723
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, they had been playing around there.

1283
01:18:11,824 --> 01:18:15,286
[SPEAKER_00]: But that was the first time they had gotten that noisy.

1284
01:18:15,867 --> 01:18:18,809
[SPEAKER_00]: Because I'm sure the dog barking will be on the tape.

1285
01:18:19,270 --> 01:18:21,832
[SPEAKER_00]: And there was another dog that was barking farther along.

1286
01:18:21,852 --> 01:18:23,573
[SPEAKER_00]: And then the kids came up near the house.

1287
01:18:24,194 --> 01:18:25,615
[SPEAKER_00]: And the dogs started to bark.

1288
01:18:26,461 --> 01:18:29,863
[SPEAKER_01]: But nothing as distracting as that racket with the symbols, though.

1289
01:18:31,324 --> 01:18:34,826
[SPEAKER_01]: It was about a quarter to 12, I guess, when we shut the recorder off.

1290
01:18:35,687 --> 01:18:39,509
[SPEAKER_01]: And I was taking some pictures with the 35 millimeter camera.

1291
01:18:39,549 --> 01:18:43,471
[SPEAKER_01]: But they were against the back wall of the garage.

1292
01:18:43,511 --> 01:18:45,292
[SPEAKER_01]: And the light was reflecting in through the door.

1293
01:18:45,332 --> 01:18:47,114
[SPEAKER_01]: And they blew a glare off the picture.

1294
01:18:47,134 --> 01:18:48,975
[SPEAKER_01]: So I couldn't do much of that.

1295
01:18:48,995 --> 01:18:52,677
[SPEAKER_01]: Then he dug up some pictures from one of those packages in my bookcase there.

1296
01:18:53,346 --> 01:18:55,807
[SPEAKER_01]: I took a few of those, then I ran out of film.

1297
01:18:55,967 --> 01:19:04,350
[SPEAKER_01]: I thought I had a 36 exposure packet, but it turned out to be 20, and I only got about three or four pictures.

1298
01:19:04,791 --> 01:19:17,056
[SPEAKER_01]: There's a nice one he had of himself with somebody spilled coffee all over, not on his face, but I took a picture of that with the regular instant camera, so maybe that'll come out.

1299
01:19:17,756 --> 01:19:19,457
[SPEAKER_01]: So there wasn't much in the way of pictures.

1300
01:19:21,369 --> 01:19:27,472
[SPEAKER_01]: picture of himself, a kind of a head and shoulder shot, 8 by 10, when he managed the fillies, which was not what I wanted.

1301
01:19:27,512 --> 01:19:32,434
[SPEAKER_01]: I wanted an action shot earlier in his prime, but I couldn't quibble about that.

1302
01:19:32,454 --> 01:19:33,414
[SPEAKER_01]: He did autograph it.

1303
01:19:33,695 --> 01:19:34,895
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to take what you can get.

1304
01:19:35,435 --> 01:19:37,956
[SPEAKER_01]: He autographed the ball.

1305
01:19:37,996 --> 01:19:38,897
[SPEAKER_01]: Rita took a couple.

1306
01:19:39,477 --> 01:19:50,322
[SPEAKER_01]: I took a picture of him holding his 200th victory ball, and she took a picture of us just standing aimlessly there in the back of the garage.

1307
01:19:54,059 --> 01:19:57,040
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I hope the flash didn't go off in the mirror of that thing.

1308
01:19:57,100 --> 01:19:58,420
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I wondered about.

1309
01:19:59,040 --> 01:19:59,980
[SPEAKER_00]: Which picture is that?

1310
01:20:01,380 --> 01:20:02,141
[SPEAKER_01]: The one of us?

1311
01:20:02,601 --> 01:20:02,941
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

1312
01:20:02,961 --> 01:20:05,081
[SPEAKER_00]: Didn't you use a flash on him, too?

1313
01:20:05,121 --> 01:20:05,401
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

1314
01:20:06,821 --> 01:20:07,382
[SPEAKER_01]: In what mirror?

1315
01:20:07,862 --> 01:20:09,342
[SPEAKER_00]: The mirror in that sideboard.

1316
01:20:13,723 --> 01:20:16,043
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, we can't worry about that now.

1317
01:20:17,444 --> 01:20:19,444
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, well, we left there about,

1318
01:20:20,162 --> 01:20:22,103
[SPEAKER_01]: 12, 15, and then the fun began.

1319
01:20:22,123 --> 01:20:22,924
[SPEAKER_01]: I thought it was a good interview.

1320
01:20:22,944 --> 01:20:24,625
[SPEAKER_01]: I was feeling pretty good when we were coming down there.

1321
01:20:25,386 --> 01:20:30,790
[SPEAKER_01]: Went into town and stopped and got organized and started driving back.

1322
01:20:30,810 --> 01:20:42,979
[SPEAKER_01]: We were going to go to, the plan was to go up around Bakersfield today, get a start on the northern route, and maybe camp or get a motel or something there.

1323
01:20:43,019 --> 01:20:45,000
[SPEAKER_01]: But we weren't going to battle Los Angeles.

1324
01:20:45,020 --> 01:20:46,221
[SPEAKER_01]: We decided against that.

1325
01:20:47,910 --> 01:20:56,516
[SPEAKER_01]: There'd be some good interviews there, but just the traffic and trying to get a place to stay and trying to arrange these interviews, which is very hard work.

1326
01:20:58,137 --> 01:21:01,379
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's a good deal of driving to and to obscure places.

1327
01:21:03,080 --> 01:21:04,141
[SPEAKER_01]: So it just wasn't worth it.

1328
01:21:04,361 --> 01:21:09,084
[SPEAKER_01]: But then coming back down that hill from Yucca Valley, the car just wasn't working right.

1329
01:21:10,085 --> 01:21:12,026
[SPEAKER_01]: It wouldn't shift into high gear at all.

1330
01:21:13,387 --> 01:21:15,689
[SPEAKER_01]: It had trouble shifting into second gear sometimes.

1331
01:21:16,612 --> 01:21:23,035
[SPEAKER_01]: And so I just had to kind of take it about 45 miles an hour without racing it too much all the way down.

1332
01:21:23,055 --> 01:21:27,757
[SPEAKER_01]: We thought we'd go to Banning, but then we decided to go to try Palm Springs, because it's closer.

1333
01:21:27,897 --> 01:21:28,797
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a bigger town.

1334
01:21:29,678 --> 01:21:36,301
[SPEAKER_01]: And since it's such a resort town, we figured they would surely have a Ford agency there.

1335
01:21:36,361 --> 01:21:40,643
[SPEAKER_01]: So we kind of limped into Palm Springs, and hot.

1336
01:21:40,663 --> 01:21:42,824
[SPEAKER_01]: It was about 110 already by 1 o'clock.

1337
01:21:43,584 --> 01:21:44,384
[SPEAKER_01]: So we got there.

1338
01:21:46,617 --> 01:21:51,118
[SPEAKER_01]: I didn't have the air conditioner on because of all the stops in the streets there.

1339
01:21:52,498 --> 01:21:56,259
[SPEAKER_01]: And I stopped and asked the Texaco man if they had the Fort Agency.

1340
01:21:56,279 --> 01:21:58,480
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, yes, about five and a half miles down the road.

1341
01:21:58,500 --> 01:22:02,980
[SPEAKER_01]: It turned out to be a couple miles right beyond where Leo DeRocher lives.

1342
01:22:04,161 --> 01:22:11,242
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course, I said before when I was there yesterday I couldn't get back in time to see him, but that may be altered now.

1343
01:22:11,282 --> 01:22:11,982
[SPEAKER_01]: You'll find out.

1344
01:22:12,622 --> 01:22:14,723
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, we found the Fort Agency.

1345
01:22:15,867 --> 01:22:22,090
[SPEAKER_01]: He took it in, and he was booked at all kinds of jobs up through Tuesday or so.

1346
01:22:22,110 --> 01:22:25,713
[SPEAKER_01]: He had a table full of orders.

1347
01:22:27,353 --> 01:22:35,878
[SPEAKER_01]: And I told him the problem, and one of his lieutenants looked under the hood and put a smokeboard out of something, and he shut the hood down again.

1348
01:22:36,959 --> 01:22:40,101
[SPEAKER_01]: And he wanted to drive it himself, so he drove around the block or so.

1349
01:22:40,121 --> 01:22:42,542
[SPEAKER_01]: And on the way back, he says, it's got to come out.

1350
01:22:45,573 --> 01:22:46,293
[SPEAKER_01]: got to come out.

1351
01:22:49,234 --> 01:22:54,616
[SPEAKER_02]: As it was, jumping from first to third was just missing.

1352
01:22:56,376 --> 01:23:09,801
[SPEAKER_01]: While it could be driven locally in short distances, maybe even on the road it wouldn't matter much, but a lot of driving would be not suitable for it.

1353
01:23:11,601 --> 01:23:12,982
[SPEAKER_01]: So what could we do?

1354
01:23:14,446 --> 01:23:15,326
[SPEAKER_01]: 1.30 by now.

1355
01:23:15,346 --> 01:23:17,387
[SPEAKER_01]: They're booked up through Monday or Tuesday.

1356
01:23:18,287 --> 01:23:23,088
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course, everything like this happens on a Friday when they can't get in.

1357
01:23:23,428 --> 01:23:24,769
[SPEAKER_01]: They're closed Saturday and Sunday.

1358
01:23:26,449 --> 01:23:31,330
[SPEAKER_01]: So we called up to Indio, about 30 miles away.

1359
01:23:31,390 --> 01:23:32,730
[SPEAKER_01]: He's booked up till Wednesday.

1360
01:23:33,291 --> 01:23:39,492
[SPEAKER_01]: We thought of going back to Banning, but that guy's the one who said it was all right this morning when he checked the linkage.

1361
01:23:40,768 --> 01:23:42,488
[SPEAKER_01]: So we didn't know if he would know what to do about it.

1362
01:23:42,608 --> 01:23:44,089
[SPEAKER_01]: Maybe he just didn't want to bother with it.

1363
01:23:45,689 --> 01:23:57,371
[SPEAKER_01]: So we fiddled around and finally decided to just stay here, get a motel, make the best of it for the weekend and beyond, and take the thing in on Monday.

1364
01:23:57,411 --> 01:23:58,532
[SPEAKER_01]: He said bring it in early Monday.

1365
01:23:58,572 --> 01:23:59,592
[SPEAKER_01]: Maybe they'd get at it then.

1366
01:24:00,472 --> 01:24:06,493
[SPEAKER_01]: Going to be an all-day job, covered by our warranty, naturally, because we haven't gone 12,000 miles yet.

1367
01:24:07,993 --> 01:24:10,354
[SPEAKER_01]: And just to make ourselves comfortable.

1368
01:24:11,762 --> 01:24:13,023
[SPEAKER_00]: And the chap was very nice.

1369
01:24:13,063 --> 01:24:25,669
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, their service manager there, or the assistant service manager, I guess he was, he called up this hotel, this Beckley Motel, which he thought would be reasonably priced and not too far from the agency's shop there.

1370
01:24:26,390 --> 01:24:27,690
[SPEAKER_01]: And they did have a vacancy.

1371
01:24:27,710 --> 01:24:31,352
[SPEAKER_01]: And so I talked to the lady, and we came on down here.

1372
01:24:32,273 --> 01:24:34,774
[SPEAKER_01]: Got here about 2 o'clock or so and checked in.

1373
01:24:35,814 --> 01:24:36,655
[SPEAKER_01]: Have a nice room.

1374
01:24:39,882 --> 01:24:41,682
[SPEAKER_01]: The air conditioner is out in the adjoining room.

1375
01:24:41,722 --> 01:24:43,083
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't get in here very much.

1376
01:24:43,803 --> 01:24:49,346
[SPEAKER_01]: It's kind of hot in the living room here, where the sleeping quarters and TV and everything else is.

1377
01:24:49,406 --> 01:24:53,728
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's still cooler than it is outside, as we find every time we go out to bring something into the car.

1378
01:24:54,248 --> 01:24:55,328
[SPEAKER_01]: And there are many of those times.

1379
01:24:56,749 --> 01:24:58,410
[SPEAKER_01]: So our plan is to just stay here.

1380
01:24:59,610 --> 01:25:03,052
[SPEAKER_01]: And maybe I could get the Leo DeRoche interview in now, since he'll be back tomorrow.

1381
01:25:04,950 --> 01:25:08,353
[SPEAKER_01]: either see him or leave a message for me to reach him on Sunday.

1382
01:25:08,954 --> 01:25:09,774
[SPEAKER_00]: I'd leave a message.

1383
01:25:09,834 --> 01:25:10,875
[SPEAKER_01]: Or later, Saturday.

1384
01:25:11,976 --> 01:25:13,217
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

1385
01:25:13,337 --> 01:25:19,763
[SPEAKER_01]: And then on Monday, we'll take the car in, and I suppose come back here and just sit around.

1386
01:25:20,604 --> 01:25:21,985
[SPEAKER_00]: They said they'd drive you back.

1387
01:25:22,025 --> 01:25:22,345
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

1388
01:25:23,566 --> 01:25:24,807
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's pretty good here.

1389
01:25:24,827 --> 01:25:28,731
[SPEAKER_01]: We've got plenty of supplies, TV, the baseball game is just on.

1390
01:25:28,771 --> 01:25:32,614
[SPEAKER_01]: It's over, and there'll be a couple tomorrow, one Sunday and one Monday.

1391
01:25:33,679 --> 01:25:35,380
[SPEAKER_01]: There'd be baseball every day to watch here.

1392
01:25:37,220 --> 01:25:40,942
[SPEAKER_01]: And there'd be books to read and catching up to do generally.

1393
01:25:42,242 --> 01:25:43,863
[SPEAKER_01]: So it was quite a blow to hear this.

1394
01:25:44,043 --> 01:25:49,245
[SPEAKER_01]: But we expected, I guess, something would happen on this trip.

1395
01:25:49,385 --> 01:25:50,465
[SPEAKER_01]: We didn't know what it would be.

1396
01:25:51,706 --> 01:25:54,247
[SPEAKER_01]: And I don't think we thought that would be it.

1397
01:25:55,267 --> 01:25:56,848
[SPEAKER_00]: Could have happened in a worse place.

1398
01:25:57,228 --> 01:26:03,270
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, we're thinking what would happen if that began to act up in the heat of the desert.

1399
01:26:03,838 --> 01:26:06,000
[SPEAKER_01]: We crossed over yesterday 300 miles.

1400
01:26:06,900 --> 01:26:08,662
[SPEAKER_01]: And we were below sea level to some places.

1401
01:26:10,023 --> 01:26:17,669
[SPEAKER_01]: But after the drive was rather smooth, the car behaved until we got here, where at least we have a place now.

1402
01:26:19,130 --> 01:26:20,010
[SPEAKER_01]: Can't get it fixed.

1403
01:26:20,891 --> 01:26:24,454
[SPEAKER_00]: I was thinking, here, Indio is all filled up.

1404
01:26:25,054 --> 01:26:31,299
[SPEAKER_00]: Here, Palm Springs, the Ford dealer, is so busy he doesn't know what to do.

1405
01:26:31,806 --> 01:26:33,826
[SPEAKER_00]: And up at Banning, they didn't have anything.

1406
01:26:35,327 --> 01:26:36,847
[SPEAKER_01]: And we think we know why now.

1407
01:26:37,347 --> 01:26:37,947
[SPEAKER_00]: That's right.

1408
01:26:38,888 --> 01:26:39,788
[SPEAKER_01]: He doesn't know anything.

1409
01:26:39,808 --> 01:26:41,008
[SPEAKER_01]: I wanted to really change the oil.

1410
01:26:42,348 --> 01:26:43,609
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it was $26.

1411
01:26:45,069 --> 01:26:48,150
[SPEAKER_01]: We might mention that to them.

1412
01:26:48,850 --> 01:26:49,510
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

1413
01:26:49,670 --> 01:26:52,691
[SPEAKER_01]: So it was quite a blow, and we're going to lose three or four days here.

1414
01:26:54,031 --> 01:26:58,592
[SPEAKER_01]: We might pick up an interview and maybe get caught up in our reading.

1415
01:26:59,042 --> 01:27:04,224
[SPEAKER_01]: Maybe I can get a swimming suit and cool off in the pool, but I doubt there's any place to get one.

1416
01:27:06,926 --> 01:27:10,007
[SPEAKER_00]: But we are not upset.

1417
01:27:10,807 --> 01:27:12,928
[SPEAKER_01]: We are taking it all in our stride.

1418
01:27:12,948 --> 01:27:17,530
[SPEAKER_01]: We've begun our fifth week of this vacation in quite a stylish way.

1419
01:27:17,931 --> 01:27:18,251
[SPEAKER_00]: Yep.

1420
01:27:20,092 --> 01:27:25,414
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if we're frustrated, we're... Any thought of going to L.A. now is certainly

1421
01:27:27,170 --> 01:27:28,671
[SPEAKER_01]: killed by this development.

1422
01:27:28,791 --> 01:27:30,311
[SPEAKER_01]: They just wouldn't have the time.

1423
01:27:30,952 --> 01:27:37,415
[SPEAKER_01]: We have, I think, a very deep-seated feeling to get out of Southern California as fast as we can.

1424
01:27:37,695 --> 01:27:38,375
[SPEAKER_00]: And you're right.

1425
01:27:38,795 --> 01:27:39,435
[SPEAKER_01]: Once we can.

1426
01:27:41,536 --> 01:27:42,057
[SPEAKER_00]: You're right.

1427
01:27:42,277 --> 01:27:44,018
[SPEAKER_01]: It's hot here, but it's not up in Banning.

1428
01:27:44,118 --> 01:27:46,679
[SPEAKER_01]: It's about 10 to 15 degrees cooler there.

1429
01:27:46,699 --> 01:27:51,301
[SPEAKER_01]: It's quite interesting how that elevation makes that much difference.

1430
01:27:51,381 --> 01:27:54,002
[SPEAKER_01]: And LA itself, I guess, is never nearly as hot as it is here.

1431
01:27:54,311 --> 01:28:00,994
[SPEAKER_01]: I've heard in the news many times the hottest place in the country has been Palm Springs, California, or Phoenix, Arizona.

1432
01:28:01,034 --> 01:28:04,575
[SPEAKER_01]: Those two places seem to take the cake.

1433
01:28:04,675 --> 01:28:06,336
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can see why it is now.

1434
01:28:07,256 --> 01:28:21,102
[SPEAKER_01]: This is a real plush community, fabulous apartment, a living apartment with facilities, with pools in there, all their spas and jacuzzis.

1435
01:28:21,242 --> 01:28:23,063
[SPEAKER_01]: And the streets of the town are lined

1436
01:28:23,779 --> 01:28:24,799
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like Fifth Avenue.

1437
01:28:24,839 --> 01:28:26,260
[SPEAKER_01]: Saks Fifth Avenue is there.

1438
01:28:26,320 --> 01:28:31,042
[SPEAKER_01]: I saw one of the broker's offices and all the sub-broker offices.

1439
01:28:32,503 --> 01:28:40,146
[SPEAKER_01]: So it's catering to the elite, without a question, who retire to this area, or else spending the sub-months here.

1440
01:28:40,386 --> 01:28:45,228
[SPEAKER_00]: There's an Allen Ladd building and a Frank Sinatra Boulevard and a Bob Hope Boulevard.

1441
01:28:45,248 --> 01:28:46,509
[SPEAKER_00]: Gene Autry Motel.

1442
01:28:47,589 --> 01:28:48,490
[SPEAKER_00]: It charges $80 a day.

1443
01:28:51,626 --> 01:28:56,728
[SPEAKER_01]: A lot of these places close up in the next month or so because people will stay here in this hot weather.

1444
01:28:58,468 --> 01:29:00,049
[SPEAKER_01]: I guess it gets even hotter in July.

1445
01:29:00,569 --> 01:29:01,529
[SPEAKER_01]: It's going to be pretty hot.

1446
01:29:02,289 --> 01:29:03,410
[SPEAKER_01]: We're just filling time here.

1447
01:29:03,430 --> 01:29:04,750
[SPEAKER_01]: This tape ought to run out by now.

1448
01:29:04,810 --> 01:29:10,372
[SPEAKER_01]: I can't understand why it keeps on going.

1449
01:29:10,412 --> 01:29:11,092
[SPEAKER_00]: But it is.

1450
01:29:12,332 --> 01:29:13,713
[SPEAKER_01]: Let's see how much there is left.

1451
01:29:16,654 --> 01:29:17,794
[SPEAKER_01]: There's not very much left.

1452
01:29:18,997 --> 01:29:20,838
[SPEAKER_01]: So we might as well use it up and tell jokes.

1453
01:29:21,118 --> 01:29:22,759
[SPEAKER_01]: What kind of jokes?

1454
01:29:23,940 --> 01:29:25,001
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, good jokes.

1455
01:29:25,121 --> 01:29:26,442
[SPEAKER_01]: How about some Polish jokes?

1456
01:29:26,982 --> 01:29:27,282
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, no.

1457
01:29:27,302 --> 01:29:32,745
[SPEAKER_01]: We went to breakfast this morning at Carl's in Banning.

1458
01:29:32,805 --> 01:29:35,747
[SPEAKER_01]: It's kind of a cafeteria or yard or something.

1459
01:29:36,167 --> 01:29:37,108
[SPEAKER_01]: They bring it to you.

1460
01:29:37,989 --> 01:29:41,811
[SPEAKER_01]: And a rather bouncy, blonde young lady waited on us.

1461
01:29:42,091 --> 01:29:46,834
[SPEAKER_01]: And she cashed the traveler's check and said, West Virginia.

1462
01:29:47,665 --> 01:29:49,206
[SPEAKER_01]: Where are you from in West Virginia?

1463
01:29:49,826 --> 01:29:51,127
[SPEAKER_01]: We said, Parkersburg.

1464
01:29:51,607 --> 01:29:52,668
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm from Charleston.

1465
01:29:54,568 --> 01:29:55,609
[SPEAKER_01]: How'd you get to Charleston?

1466
01:29:55,629 --> 01:29:59,511
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, we left when I was 13, which must have been about four or five years ago.

1467
01:30:00,491 --> 01:30:01,172
[SPEAKER_01]: She wasn't that old.

1468
01:30:01,192 --> 01:30:02,252
[SPEAKER_01]: Maybe she was in her early 20s.

1469
01:30:02,653 --> 01:30:04,914
[SPEAKER_01]: I went to Florida, and then from Florida they came out here.

1470
01:30:06,575 --> 01:30:07,435
[SPEAKER_01]: She was a good one, though.

1471
01:30:08,916 --> 01:30:11,157
[SPEAKER_01]: And then they had a crisis, though, at Carl's.

1472
01:30:12,117 --> 01:30:14,058
[SPEAKER_01]: Somebody didn't show up, and somebody else showed up late.

1473
01:30:14,979 --> 01:30:17,380
[SPEAKER_01]: I know when the manager came in, this blonde said to her,

1474
01:30:18,265 --> 01:30:19,446
[SPEAKER_01]: Are you in a good mood today?"

1475
01:30:20,526 --> 01:30:21,547
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, yes.

1476
01:30:21,667 --> 01:30:22,647
[SPEAKER_01]: I said, we're shorthanded.

1477
01:30:22,667 --> 01:30:28,350
[SPEAKER_02]: It was a good breakfast there.

1478
01:30:28,370 --> 01:30:28,990
[SPEAKER_01]: We enjoyed it.

1479
01:30:30,651 --> 01:30:32,292
[SPEAKER_01]: We went back to pick up our car.

1480
01:30:32,372 --> 01:30:36,154
[SPEAKER_01]: Things seemed to be working fine on our way to a good interview and then off to Bakersfield.

1481
01:30:37,114 --> 01:30:38,355
[SPEAKER_01]: Things seemed pretty good then.

1482
01:30:40,535 --> 01:30:48,280
[SPEAKER_01]: All during the interview, though, I began to think, because the car had acted up a little bit on the way up, in the back of my mind, is this thing going to be a problem on the way out of here?

1483
01:30:49,960 --> 01:30:57,765
[SPEAKER_01]: And the answer was yes, it was a problem.

1484
01:30:57,785 --> 01:31:03,348
[SPEAKER_01]: And since you can't think of any more jokes, and we want to watch Wonder Woman.

1485
01:31:03,608 --> 01:31:07,090
[SPEAKER_01]: It's almost over now, but we can watch the last few minutes

1486
01:31:07,706 --> 01:31:12,410
[SPEAKER_01]: We could turn Wonder Woman back up just in time to see her heroics.

1487
01:31:16,052 --> 01:31:18,975
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's about it.

1488
01:31:19,535 --> 01:31:20,596
[SPEAKER_01]: I wish this tape would end.

1489
01:31:21,657 --> 01:31:22,938
[SPEAKER_00]: And it just keeps on going?

1490
01:31:23,838 --> 01:31:24,018
[SPEAKER_01]: End.

1491
01:31:27,501 --> 01:31:29,463
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll give this tape ten seconds to end.

1492
01:31:30,844 --> 01:31:30,964
[SPEAKER_02]: One.

1493
01:31:32,405 --> 01:31:32,725
[SPEAKER_01]: Two.

1494
01:31:34,206 --> 01:31:34,606
[SPEAKER_01]: Three.

1495
01:31:36,228 --> 01:31:36,568
[SPEAKER_01]: Four.

1496
01:31:38,233 --> 01:31:49,575
[SPEAKER_01]: 5 6 7 7 1⁄2 8 8 1⁄4 8 1⁄2 8 3⁄4 9 9 1⁄8 9 1⁄4 9 3⁄8 9 7 1⁄16

1497
01:32:08,810 --> 01:32:09,450
[SPEAKER_01]: Nine and a half.

1498
01:32:11,092 --> 01:32:12,352
[SPEAKER_01]: Nine and nine-sixteenths.

1499
01:32:13,873 --> 01:32:16,315
[SPEAKER_01]: Nine and five-eighths.

1500
01:32:17,356 --> 01:32:18,316
[SPEAKER_01]: Nine and what's next?

1501
01:32:19,297 --> 01:32:20,498
[SPEAKER_02]: Nine eleven-sixteenths.

1502
01:32:22,259 --> 01:32:25,601
[SPEAKER_01]: Nine and three quarters.

1503
01:32:28,563 --> 01:32:31,965
[SPEAKER_01]: Nine and thirteen-sixteenths.

1504
01:32:34,947 --> 01:32:37,109
[SPEAKER_01]: Nine and seven-eighths.

1505
01:32:40,120 --> 01:32:42,283
[SPEAKER_01]: 9 and 15 sixteenths.

1506
01:32:45,207 --> 01:32:46,810
[SPEAKER_00]: Is that going to do it for you, Father?

1507
01:32:53,700 --> 01:32:54,661
[SPEAKER_00]: That is for sure.

